\
Scale Factor - How do I set it?\
How you set the scale factor depends on what you want to achieve by tracking your power during a workout. If you want the true work being done at the handle note that resulting power will be different than that of the rowing machine because they are simply different motions in a number of ways. If you prefer to pSplit or power values on the water to be as close as possible to the rowing machine the best way to do that is to use your heart rate to calibrate it or perhaps a max intensity piece.  \
\
It is perfectly acceptable to think of the scale factor as just like a calibration factor you would use to get the correct speed from an impeller based system.   \
\
\
If one\'92s goal is to get the true work being done  at the oar handle you will find that your measured power output is likely going to be different from that of a rowing machine because the motion of the body to perform a rowing stroke (oar handle rotating about a fulcrum) is different than the straight motion on the rowing machine. Another example: anyone who has tracked their power output on a bike erg vs rower erg will likely see a difference there as well. Before you set about measuring to get a scale factor for true work / power consider if that\'92s what you really want for training \
\
\
Understanding Physics of effort.\
\
\
\
It is useful to understand how force, power and work are different units of measurement in describing the physics of boat propulsion.  For example let\'92s visualize a scenario where force is exerted but there is no power or work.  Imagine a rower sitting at the start and also that the stern or bow of the boat is held securely enough that the rower cannot free the boat no matter how hard they try.  Also imagine that the blade of the oar is pushing against a fixed piling.  The rower can exert as much force as they want against the foot stretcher and the handle and no work is done to the boat because the oar handle does not move and neither does the boat. The muscles of the rower may beg to differ of course because it takes energy to maintain that force however none of it is being transferred to the boat. We capture this force by measuring the difference in rotation between the locations of the sensors (which is bend).  \
\
Work = Force * Distance.  So in our system in order to measure work the piling has to be removed so that \
\
Work is force * distance so once piling is magically removed, the rower can do work by moving the handle during the drive.  In our case we measure the \'93distance\'94 as the arc of motion of the handle (eg stroke length = 95degrees).   So for each stroke we can measure the force exerted \
\
\
\
\
Now let\'92s consider the normal rowing situation (no piling for blade, no fixed boat).  Work is the energy the rower exerts during the drive phase of the stroke and we calculate using Force * Distance.  We get the applied force to the oar by measuring the bend or difference in rotation in the locations where the sensors are placed (and multiply that by a scale factor which accounts for the distance between the sensors, stiffness of the oar, inboard etc).  }